          "label": "<p>At the transpyloric plane (L1), what is the correct anterior-to-posterior sequen</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>At the transpyloric plane (L1), what is the correct anterior-to-posterior sequence of structures overlying the aorta?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stomach → pancreatic neck → superior mesenteric artery → superior mesenteric vein → aorta</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The superior mesenteric vein lies anterior to the superior mesenteric artery at this level. The SMV is closer to the pancreatic neck, not behind the SMA.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stomach → pancreatic neck → superior mesenteric vein → superior mesenteric artery → aorta</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. From front to back: stomach, then pancreatic neck overlying the SMV, then the SMA, and finally the aorta posteriorly.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Pancreatic neck → stomach → superior mesenteric vein → superior mesenteric artery → aorta</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The stomach is intraperitoneal and lies anterior to the retroperitoneal pancreas. The pancreatic neck cannot be anterior to the stomach.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Stomach → superior mesenteric vein → pancreatic neck → superior mesenteric artery → aorta</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The pancreatic neck overlies and is anterior to the SMV. The neck sits on top of the portal-mesenteric venous confluence, not behind it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct sequence at L1 from front to back is: stomach → pancreatic neck → SMV → SMA → aorta. This layering explains why pancreatic tumours can encase or compress the major mesenteric vessels."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "This layered relationship is critical during pancreatic surgery — the neck of the pancreas sits directly over the portal-splenic-mesenteric venous confluence, making dissection at this plane hazardous."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
