          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In a woman with a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, where does free blood first accumu</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In a woman with a ruptured ectopic pregnancy, where does free blood first accumulate in the peritoneal cavity?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vesicouterine pouch</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The vesicouterine pouch between the bladder and uterus is a shallow recess. It is not the most dependent point in the female pelvis, so fluid gravitates elsewhere first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Retropubic space (cave of Retzius)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The retropubic space is an extraperitoneal compartment between the pubic symphysis and bladder. Free intraperitoneal fluid cannot collect in this space.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas)</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The pouch of Douglas is the most dependent recess in the female peritoneal cavity, so gravity causes free blood to pool here first.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Paravesical fossa</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The paravesical fossa lies lateral to the bladder and is not the lowest point of the female peritoneal cavity. Fluid gravitates to the deeper rectouterine pouch.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The rectouterine pouch (pouch of Douglas) is the most dependent peritoneal recess in the female pelvis. Free blood from a ruptured ectopic pregnancy gravitates here and is detectable on transvaginal ultrasound as echogenic free fluid."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The pouch of Douglas is the lowest point of the female peritoneal cavity. Blood from a ruptured ectopic pregnancy collects here and appears as free fluid on transvaginal ultrasound. It can be sampled by culdocentesis — a needle passed through the posterior vaginal fornix."
                }
              ],
